# Jev vs Frontier LLM Demo

A live workshop-style Next.js demo that compares a probability-aware “Jev” classifier against a frontier LLM on the same customer-support email classification task.

The app presents the same emails, the same three options, and the same machine to both models, then surfaces differences in speed, cost, confidence, calibration, and failure patterns.

## Why this project exists

This repo is designed to demonstrate a practical question in AI evaluation:

- When two models solve the same classification task, how do they differ?
- Does one model produce more calibrated confidence?
- Can a structured model make more reliable decisions than a general chat model?
- How do latency, retries, and provider behavior affect a production decision pipeline?

The demo uses support emails from an online appliance store and classifies customer intent into:

- complaint
- refund_request
- cancel_account

See `config/task.yaml` for the canonical task definition.

## Features

- Side-by-side model comparisons in a race view
- Confidence analysis for each prediction
- Stress-test interface for adversarial or difficult emails
- Calibration view for evaluating model certainty vs. correctness
- Dataset browser for reviewing the synthetic email corpus
- Health checks for both model APIs and local setup checks
- Replay support for saved race runs

## Stack

- Next.js
- React
- TypeScript
- Vitest
- OpenAI-compatible API access via OpenRouter
- Typesafe AI SDK for Jev integration

